Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be either giants or dwarfs; alone it is not a strong indicator.  
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star; distance alone does not distinguish giants from dwarfs.  
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Measurement uncertainty; high error may reduce confidence in distance-dependent features.  
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature; giants can have a wide range of B-V, but very blue or very red colors may be typical for giants.  
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class suffix (e.g., V = dwarf, III = giant, II = bright giant, Ib = supergiant) is the best direct indicator of giant status.  
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants are more luminous than dwarfs, so lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes indicate giants.  

Step 2. Based on the above and examples, 10 different conditions for each class:  

10 different conditions for class "0" (not giant):  
- SpType is in [G1V, F3V, F5V, A1V, G3/G5V, F0V, A9V, G3V, B1V:nn, F0V, A1V, G2V, K5V] (any spectral type with luminosity class V)  
- Amag >= 15.5  
- B-V is within range of [0.0, 0.7]  
- Plx >= 3.0  
- Vmag >= 5.5  
- e_Plx <= 1.1  
- Amag >= 16.0 and Amag <= 19.5  
- B-V <= 0.7  
- SpType is in [A1V, F0V, G3V, K5V]  
- Plx is within range of [3.0, 20.0]  

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant):  
- SpType is in [K0III, G7III, A2III, K1III, G8III, B1Ib/II, K2/K3III, B7II, G5III, K3III] (any spectral type with luminosity class III or II or Ib)  
- Amag < 16.0  
- B-V >= 0.9  
- Plx <= 7.5  
- Vmag <= 8.5  
- e_Plx <= 1.2  
- Amag is within range of [13.0, 16.0]  
- B-V is within range of [0.9, 1.3]  
- SpType is in [K0III, G7III, A2III, K1III, G8III, B1Ib/II, K2/K3III, B7II]  
- Plx is within range of [0.8, 7.5]

---DIVIDER---

Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be either giants or dwarfs depending on distance; alone, Vmag is not a strong indicator without distance.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star; giants tend to be luminous and thus can be seen at larger distances with smaller parallaxes.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Measurement uncertainty; high error reduces reliability of distance and thus absolute magnitude estimation.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature and spectral type; giants often have different B-V ranges compared to dwarfs of the same spectral class.
- SpType (spectral type): Luminosity class indicated by suffix (V = dwarf/main sequence, III = giant, II = bright giant, Ib = supergiant); this is the most direct indicator of giant status.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower absolute magnitudes (brighter intrinsically) than dwarfs; higher Amag values indicate fainter stars, so giants have smaller or moderate Amag values compared to dwarfs at same spectral type.

Step 2. Based on the examples and Step 1, 10 conditions for each class:

10 different conditions for class "0" (not a giant star):
- SpType is in [any spectral type ending with "V" e.g. G1V, F3V, A1V, B1V:nn, K5V]
- Amag is greater than 15
- Plx is greater than 5
- B-V is less than 0.7
- Vmag is greater than or equal to 5
- Amag is within range of [16, 20]
- Plx is within range of [5, 20]
- e_Plx is less than or equal to 1.1
- B-V is within range of [0.1, 0.7]
- SpType is not in [III, II, Ib, Ib/II, III/IV] luminosity classes

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant star):
- SpType is in [any spectral type containing "III" or "II" or "Ib" or "Ib/II" e.g. K0III, G8III, B7II, B1Ib/II]
- Amag is less than or equal to 16
- Plx is less than 5
- B-V is greater than 0.7
- Vmag is less than 9
- Amag is within range of [13, 16]
- Plx is within range of [0, 5]
- e_Plx is less than or equal to 1.2
- B-V is within range of [0.8, 1.3]
- SpType is not in [V] luminosity class

These conditions are consistent with the examples where giants have spectral types with luminosity class III or II or Ib, have smaller parallaxes (farther away), brighter absolute magnitudes (smaller Amag), and generally redder colors (higher B-V). Non-giants are mostly main sequence stars (class V) with larger parallaxes (closer), fainter absolute magnitudes, and bluer or moderate B-V values.

---DIVIDER---

