Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:  
- Vmag (apparent magnitude) indicates how bright a star appears from Earth, but brightness alone does not distinguish giants from dwarfs because distance affects apparent brightness.  
- Plx (parallax) measures distance; smaller parallax means greater distance. Giants tend to be luminous and can be seen at larger distances yet still appear bright.  
- e_Plx (error in parallax) is measurement uncertainty, less relevant directly to classification.  
- B-V (color index) indicates the star’s temperature and spectral class; giants and dwarfs can share similar B-V values, but some spectral types are more common among giants.  
- SpType (spectral type) includes luminosity class (V for dwarfs/main sequence, III for giants, II for bright giants, Ib for supergiants). This is a strong direct indicator of whether a star is a giant or not.  
- Amag (absolute magnitude) is intrinsic brightness; giants have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes than dwarfs of the same spectral type.  
In summary, SpType and Amag are the strongest indicators of giant status, while Plx and Vmag help infer distance and apparent brightness but are less directly indicative. B-V and spectral subtypes assist in refining classification.

